WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:01.498
You found a bug.

2
00:00:01.498 --> 00:00:03.932
Your app is already on people's phones.

3
00:00:03.932 --> 00:00:05.616
Do you wait days to fix it?

4
00:00:05.616 --> 00:00:07.800
With Shipvo, you fix it in minutes.

5
00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:11.604
This is called an OTA update.

6
00:00:11.604 --> 00:00:13.382
OTA means over the air.

7
00:00:13.382 --> 00:00:18.075
The phone gets the new version straight from you, like a web page.

8
00:00:19.675 --> 00:00:20.277
Step one.

9
00:00:20.277 --> 00:00:21.215
Edit your app.

10
00:00:21.215 --> 00:00:25.900
In a Shipvo app, the first file to change is src/release.ts.

11
00:00:27.500 --> 00:00:28.316
Step two.

12
00:00:28.316 --> 00:00:29.766
Run one command.

13
00:00:29.766 --> 00:00:31.125
shipvo publish.

14
00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:36.994
Shipvo builds your new bundle and takes its fingerprint.

15
00:00:36.994 --> 00:00:41.175
Then it builds small patches, so phones download only what changed.

16
00:00:42.775 --> 00:00:45.826
Then it signs the release with your private key.

17
00:00:45.826 --> 00:00:47.796
That key stays on your machine.

18
00:00:47.796 --> 00:00:51.227
Phones check the signature before they apply anything.

19
00:00:51.227 --> 00:00:54.850
So a stolen publish token cannot ship code to your users.

20
00:00:56.450 --> 00:00:58.850
Releases go out in three groups.

21
00:00:58.850 --> 00:01:00.425
One percent of users.

22
00:01:00.425 --> 00:01:01.700
Then ten percent.

23
00:01:01.700 --> 00:01:02.750
Then everyone.

24
00:01:04.450 --> 00:01:09.479
If a release fails to start three times on a phone, that phone throws it away.

25
00:01:09.479 --> 00:01:15.475
And if you need to go back, one command, shipvo rollback, sends an older release to everyone.

26
00:01:17.075 --> 00:01:19.262
Back up your signing seed first.

27
00:01:19.262 --> 00:01:20.697
Then try it yourself.

28
00:01:20.697 --> 00:01:25.550
The full guide is at shipvo.dev/docs/getting-started.
